  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality\n$\\frac{5-2x}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}-3)}\\ge 0,\\,x>2.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "Since $x>2,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3 & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}-3)$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>2).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}-3),$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-2x}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}-3)} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}-3))\\cdot \\frac{5-2x}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}-3)} & \\ge (\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}-3))\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5-2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2x & \\ge -5 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign again of the inequality since we divided by a negative number ($-2$).\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>2$ and $x\\le \\frac{5}{2}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $2<x\\le \\frac{5}{2}.$",
